# Mutation cases for "the Kotlin suite runs the engine it was asked for".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/kotlin_engine_selection.cases`.
#
# The harness is read by the tests at run time in the sense that matters here —
# it is Kotlin source compiled into the suite the selector names, so it is a
# `mutation_targets`.
#
# What these guard is the sentence a CI lane says about itself. The Kotlin arm
# runs the generated machines once per engine/language pair, and a repeat is
# worth its minutes only while `-Psce.script.engine=quickjs` and "QuickJS
# evaluated the expressions" are the same statement.
#
# They were not, and the failure was silent in both directions. The selection
# ended in `else -> RhinoScriptEngine()`, so a misspelt engine was a green run
# of the default: measured, `-Psce.script.engine=nonexistent-engine` reached
# Pass on every one of them. Nothing in the suite could notice, because no
# generated case can see which engine it reached Pass on.
#
# (That sentence used to end "passed all 226 cases", and the header-counting
# check reads a number beside the word "cases" as a claim about THIS FILE's
# case count. It was neither wrong nor about this file — it counted the W3C
# suite — so the number is gone rather than corrected, which is the same repair
# the gate's own comment took when its totals kept rotting.)
#
# ⚠ The gate became `engine:language` PAIRS on 2026-08-30: this backend emits
# machines for two script-engine languages, the committed tree holds one, and
# an engine reading the other needs its own tree generated. Two of the anchors
# below moved with that change, and they had to be re-aimed rather than
# deleted — an anchor that stops matching makes its case INCONCLUSIVE, which
# reads as "nothing to report" in exactly the place a report was wanted.

# The selector names both binaries, because the population claim under attack
# here is answered in more than one of them and a selector naming a single
# binary would judge only part of it. The gate's array is held to the
# LANGUAGES this backend can emit by `gate_registry_contract`, and that check
# reads `ScriptEngineTarget::ALL` — so shrinking ALL is an escape hatch out of
# the same gate, and the test that closes it lives in
# `script_engine_target_input`.
#
# ⚠ Target selectors, never a bare filter: a filter reaches every binary the
# selector matched.
mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli \
    --test gate_registry_contract --test script_engine_target_input
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh backends/kotlin/tests/build.gradle.kts

# `generator.rs` is COMPILED INTO both binaries above, so it is a
# `mutation_targets` rather than a runtime one: the harness can hash what the
# edit produced and tell a surviving mutation from one that never reached the
# code.
mutation_targets sce-build/src/generator.rs

# ── The gate stops running the second engine ───────────────────────
#
# The shape this gate had before: a lane named for one engine, covering one
# engine, with the other reachable only by hand. It passes — it always passed
# — so the only signal that anything narrowed is the summary naming one engine
# where it used to name two.

mutation_case "the Kotlin gate covers only the default engine again" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript quickjs:ecmascript lua:ecmascript lua:lua)",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript)")
PY

# ── The lowered route loses its row ────────────────────────────────
#
# Narrower than the case above, and the one that matters most: dropping
# `lua:lua` leaves the engine still covered — `lua:ecmascript` runs it — while
# the BUILD-TIME lowering route goes unmeasured above the expression level.
# That is the exact state this gate carried as a written-down debt until
# 2026-08-30, and it reads as coverage because the engine's name is still in
# the array.
#
# Caught by `the_kotlin_gate_runs_every_language_the_generator_can_emit`: the
# generator still emits a `lua` suite, and no row would run it. Note which
# half that is — the ENGINE half of the same claim (`lua` accepts both
# languages, so both are rows) is `GateEnginePairsTest` on the JVM, and this
# runner cannot reach it. A cargo casefile can only be honest about the half
# it can judge, which is why the assertion above was written in Rust rather
# than left to the Kotlin suite alone.

mutation_case "the build-time lowering route loses its row while the engine keeps one" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript quickjs:ecmascript lua:ecmascript lua:lua)",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript quickjs:ecmascript lua:ecmascript)")
PY

# ── The population the gate check sweeps gets smaller ──────────────
#
# The escape hatch out of the case above. That check compares the gate's rows
# against the languages `Language::Kotlin` can emit for, and it enumerates
# them through `ScriptEngineTarget::ALL`. Shrink ALL and the comparison still
# runs, still passes, and asks about one language — which is a gate that
# cannot be wrong rather than a gate that is right.
#
# Worth its own case because the repair for the case above and the repair for
# this one point in opposite directions: one says "add the row", this one says
# "the population is not the array's to narrow". Both must be red, or the
# first is only red until someone finds the second.

mutation_case "the engine-language population shrinks to the committed one" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/generator.rs",
     "&[ScriptEngineTarget::EcmaScript, ScriptEngineTarget::Lua];",
     "&[ScriptEngineTarget::EcmaScript];")
PY

# ── The committed language becomes a constant again ────────────────
#
# `COMMITTED_LANGUAGE` decides which rows need their own generated tree, and it
# is read from the generator's manifest. A literal is correct today and wrong
# on the one day it has to be right — the day
# `Language::Kotlin.default_script_engine_target()` flips — at which point
# every row would be handed the wrong tree and the gate would call it a
# conformance regression.

mutation_case "the gate writes down the committed language instead of asking" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     'COMMITTED_LANGUAGE="$(',
     'COMMITTED_LANGUAGE=ecmascript  # $(')
PY

# ── The suite stops re-reading the gate ────────────────────────────
#
# `GateEnginePairsTest` holds the array to the routes this backend has, by
# reading the gate off disk. Gradle cannot see that read, so without the input
# declaration the test task stays UP-TO-DATE across every edit to the file the
# test is about — measured 2026-08-30 as BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 500ms with no test
# run, on the very edit that deleted a row.
#
# A suite cannot observe its own staleness: the run that would report it is the
# run that does not happen. So this is guarded from the Rust side.

mutation_case "the Kotlin suite stops re-reading the gate it checks" <<'PY'
edit("backends/kotlin/tests/build.gradle.kts",
     'inputs.file(File(rootDir, "scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh"))',
     'inputs.file(File(rootDir, "tests/ecmascript/ecma262_semantics.json"))')
PY

# ── The per-pair floor stops being a floor ─────────────────────────
#
# Gradle reports BUILD SUCCESSFUL for a test task it decided was UP-TO-DATE,
# so the count read from the JUnit XML is what separates a conformance result
# from a build that ran nothing. Dropping the floor for one pair leaves that
# pair's arm able to report green over an empty run.

mutation_case "a pair may report green over an empty run" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     "    if (( cases < 200 )); then",
     "    if (( cases < 0 )); then")
PY

# ── A failing pair stops failing the gate ──────────────────────────
#
# Every pair's verdict has to reach the gate's. A loop that reported each
# result and returned the last one would pass whenever the final pair passed,
# which is a pair that was already covered.

mutation_case "a failing pair is reported and then forgiven" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     'sce_gate_fail "Kotlin conformance on $engine over $language machines: $failures failure(s), $errors error(s) across $cases case(s)"',
     'sce_gate_step "Kotlin conformance on $engine over $language machines: $failures failure(s), $errors error(s) across $cases case(s)"')
PY
